e \\( 2^n \\equiv 1 \\mod 3 \\) when \\( n \\) is even).\n   - \\( 5 \\cdot 3^m \\equiv 0 \\mod 3 \\), simplifying the equation modulo 3 to \\( 1 \\equiv k^2 \\mod 3 \\), which is valid.\n\n2. **Modulo 4 Analysis**:\n   - For \\( n \\geq 2 \\), \\( 2^n \\equiv 0 \\mod 4 \\). Thus, \\( 5 \\cdot 3^m \\equiv k^2 \\mod 4 \\). Since \\( 5 \\cdot 3^m \\equiv 1 \\cdot 3^m \\mod 4 \\) and \\( 3 \\equiv -1 \\mod 4 \\), \\( 3^m \\equiv (-1)^'}]}
